<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet xml:id="lesson-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Lesson 01</title>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions">
        <title>Identifying Functions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Determine whether the given relation represents a function. If it is not a function,
                explain why not.</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions-1" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\{(1,2),(-3,3),(6,3)\}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Check if any <m>x</m>-value maps to more than one <m>y</m>-value.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Yes, it is a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Each input value (1, -3, 6) corresponds to exactly one output value. Therefore,
                    it is a function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions-2" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\{(-4,4),(8,2),(-4,7)\}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Look at the mappings for the <m>x</m>-value <m>-4</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>No, it is not a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>The input value <m>-4</m> maps to two different output values (<m>4</m> and <m>7</m>).
                    Therefore, it is not a function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions-3" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>\{(1,-9),(-8,3),(-5,7)\}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Are all first coordinates unique?</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Yes, it is a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Each input value (1, -8, -5) corresponds to exactly one output value. Therefore,
                    it is a function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions-4" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>xy-x=5</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Isolate <m>y</m> to see if a single <m>x</m> produces a single <m>y</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Yes, it is a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>xy - x \amp= 5</mrow>
                        <mrow>x(y - 1) \amp= 5</mrow>
                        <mrow>y - 1 \amp= \frac{5}{x}</mrow>
                        <mrow>y \amp= \frac{5}{x} + 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>For any valid input <m>x</m>, there is exactly one output <m>y</m>. Therefore, it
                    is a function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-determine-functions-5" workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>2x+y^4=3</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Solve the equation for <m>y</m> and check for multiple roots.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>No, it is not a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>2x + y^4 \amp= 3</mrow>
                        <mrow>y^4 \amp= 3 - 2x</mrow>
                        <mrow>y \amp= \pm\sqrt[4]{3 - 2x}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>Because of the <m>\pm</m> symbol, an input <m>x</m> can result in two different <m>
                    y</m> values. Therefore, it is not a function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-functions">
        <title>Evaluating Functions</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Evaluate the given function for the following values of the variable and simplify. If
                the function is undefined for the given value, write <em>undefined</em>.</p>
            <p>
                <ul cols="3">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f(3)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f(x-3)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>f(-x)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-functions-1" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=x^3-x^2+1</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Substitute the given value or expression into <m>x</m> and simplify the resulting
                    polynomials.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(3) = 19</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x-3) = x^3 - 10x^2 + 33x - 35</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(-x) = -x^3 - x^2 + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(3) \amp= (3)^3 - (3)^2 + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 27 - 9 + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 19</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x-3) \amp= (x-3)^3 - (x-3)^2 + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= (x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27) - (x^2 - 6x + 9) + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= x^3 - 10x^2 + 33x - 35</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= (-x)^3 - (-x)^2 + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= -x^3 - x^2 + 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-functions-2" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=2\sqrt{x+7}+1</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Substitute the given expression into the square root and combine like terms if
                    possible.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(3) = 2\sqrt{10} + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x-3) = 2\sqrt{x+4} + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(-x) = 2\sqrt{-x+7} + 1</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(3) \amp= 2\sqrt{3+7} + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 2\sqrt{10} + 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x-3) \amp= 2\sqrt{(x-3)+7} + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 2\sqrt{x+4} + 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= 2\sqrt{(-x)+7} + 1</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= 2\sqrt{-x+7} + 1</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-functions-3" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(x)=\frac{x+4}{x^2-9}</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Watch out for denominator values that equal zero.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ul>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(3) =</m>
                                <em>undefined</em>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(x-3) = \frac{x+1}{x^2-6x}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                <m>f(-x) = \frac{-x+4}{x^2-9}</m>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(3) \amp= \frac{3+4}{3^2-9}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{7}{0} \quad (\text{undefined})</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(x-3) \amp= \frac{(x-3)+4}{(x-3)^2-9}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{x+1}{(x^2-6x+9)-9}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{x+1}{x^2-6x}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    <md>
                        <mrow>f(-x) \amp= \frac{(-x)+4}{(-x)^2-9}</mrow>
                        <mrow>\amp= \frac{-x+4}{x^2-9}</mrow>
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-1-identify-functions-from-graphs">
        <title>Identifying Functions from Graphs</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Identify whether the following graph is a function. Explain why or why not.</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-identify-functions-from-graphs-1" workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-1-graph-1">
                    <caption>First Graph</caption>
                    <image width="75%">
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane displaying multiple curves. On the left side of
                                the y-axis, there are two distinct curves that overlap vertically.
                                On the right side of the y-axis, a continuous curve begins at the
                                point (3, 2) and opens to the right, forming an upper and lower
                                branch that also overlap vertically.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-lesson-1-graph-1">
                            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                                <definition>f1(x)=-4/x</definition>
                                <definition>f2(x)=x/2+2/x</definition>
                                <definition>f3(x)=sqrt(x-3)+2</definition>
                                <definition>f4(x)=-sqrt(x-3)+2</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                    <grid-axes clear-background="yes" />
                                    <graph function="f1" domain="(-5,-0.8)" thickness="2" />
                                    <graph function="f2" domain="(-5,-0.417)" thickness="2" />
                                    <graph function="f3" domain="(3,5)" thickness="2" N="300" />
                                    <graph function="f4" domain="(3,5)" thickness="2" N="300" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Imagine drawing straight vertical lines down through the graph. Does any vertical
                    line touch the curves in more than one place at the same time?</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>This graph does not represent a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>This graph does not represent a function because it fails the vertical line test.
                    There are multiple places where a vertical line will intersect the visual
                    elements more than once, meaning a single <m>x</m>-value corresponds to multiple <m>
                    y</m>-values.</p>
                <p>For example, a vertical line drawn at <m>x = 4</m> intersects the right-hand
                    curve twice (at <m>y = 1</m> and <m>y = 3</m>). Similarly, a vertical line drawn
                    at <m>x = -2</m> intersects the two separate curves on the left side of the
                    graph. Because single inputs yield multiple outputs, this relation is not a
                    function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-identify-functions-from-graphs-2" workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-1-graph-2">
                    <caption>Second Graph</caption>
                    <image width="75%">
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane displaying a graph with two separate curved
                                branches. The first branch is in the lower-left quadrant, moving
                                rightward along the negative x-axis, curving downward, and pointing
                                straight down parallel to the negative y-axis. The second branch is
                                in the upper-right quadrant, pointing straight up parallel to the
                                positive y-axis, curving rightward, and moving along the positive
                                x-axis.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-lesson-1-graph-2">
                            <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                                <definition>f(x)=2/x</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-5,-5,5,5)">
                                    <grid-axes clear-background="yes" />
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(-5,-0.4)" thickness="2" arrows="2" />
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(0.4,5)" thickness="2" arrows="2" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Imagine drawing straight vertical lines down through the graph. Does any vertical
                    line touch the curves in more than one place at the same time?</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>This graph represents a function.</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>To determine if the graph is a function, apply the vertical line test. Imagine
                    drawing vertical lines straight down through the coordinate plane.</p>
                <p>Any vertical line you draw will intersect the graph at exactly one point, except
                    at <m>x = 0</m>, where the graph has an asymptote and there is no intersection.
                    Because no single vertical line intersects the curves more than once, each <m>x</m>-value
                    corresponds to at most one <m>y</m>-value. Therefore, this graph represents a
                    function.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-from-graph">
        <title>Evaluating from a Graph</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>The graph of a function, <m>f</m>, is given. Use it to find each indicated value.</p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-piecewise-evaluation">
                <caption>Piecewise Graph for Evaluation</caption>
                <image width="75%">
                    <description>
                        <p>A coordinate plane displaying a piecewise linear graph with solid dots
                            marking specific vertices. The graph consists of connected line segments
                            starting at a point in the second quadrant, zigzagging up and down
                            across the axes, and ending with a horizontal ray pointing to the right
                            in the first quadrant. Solid dots are located at x-coordinates -8, -5,
                            -2, 0, 2, 5, and 7.</p>
                    </description>
                    <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefig-piecewise-evaluation">
                        <diagram dimensions="(400,400)" margins="5">
                            <coordinates bbox="(-10,-10,10,10)">
                                <grid hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" stroke="lightgray"
                                    spacings="((-10,1,10),(-10,1,10))" />
                                <axes hticks="(-10,1,10)" vticks="(-10,1,10)" hlabels="(-8,2,8)"
                                    vlabels="(-8,2,8)" clear-background="yes" />
                                <line p1="(-8,6)" p2="(-5,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-5,2)" p2="(-2,8)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(-2,8)" p2="(0,2)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(0,2)" p2="(2,-9)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(2,-9)" p2="(5,-5)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(5,-5)" p2="(7,1)" thickness="2" stroke="blue" />
                                <line p1="(7,1)" p2="(10,1)" thickness="2" arrows="1" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-8,6)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-5,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(-2,8)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(0,2)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(2,-9)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(5,-5)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                                <point p="(7,1)" fill="blue" size="5" stroke="blue" />
                            </coordinates>
                        </diagram>
                    </prefigure>
                </image>
            </figure>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-from-graph-1" workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(-8)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Find <m>-8</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis and locate the corresponding <m>y</m>-value
                    on the graph.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>6</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Locate <m>x = -8</m> on the horizontal axis and read the corresponding <m>y</m>-coordinate
                    from the point on the graph. The point is <m>(-8, 6)</m>, so <m>f(-8) = 6</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-from-graph-2" workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(-4)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Find <m>-4</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis and locate the corresponding <m>y</m>-value
                    on the graph. Look closely at the straight line segment connecting the points at <m>
                    x=-5</m> and <m>x=-2</m>.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>4</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Locate <m>x = -4</m> on the horizontal axis and read the corresponding <m>y</m>-coordinate
                    on the line segment connecting <m>(-5, 2)</m> and <m>(-2, 8)</m>. The line
                    passes exactly through the grid intersection at <m>y = 4</m> when <m>x = -4</m>.
                    Therefore, <m>f(-4) = 4</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-from-graph-3" workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <m>f(15)</m>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Find <m>15</m> on the <m>x</m>-axis. Notice the horizontal ray on the right side
                    of the graph and what the arrow indicates about the function's behavior for
                    large <m>x</m>-values.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>1</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>The graph features a horizontal ray that begins at <m>(7, 1)</m> and extends
                    infinitely to the right, indicated by the arrow. This means for all <m>x \ge 7</m>,
                    the output is constantly <m>1</m>. Therefore, <m>f(15) = 1</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-evaluate-from-graph-4" workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p><m>x</m> if <m>f(x)=8</m></p>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Find <m>8</m> on the <m>y</m>-axis and trace horizontally to locate the
                    corresponding <m>x</m>-value(s) on the graph.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <m>-2</m>
                </p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>Locate <m>y = 8</m> on the vertical axis, trace horizontally to the graph, and
                    read the corresponding <m>x</m>-coordinate(s). The only point on the graph that
                    reaches a height of <m>8</m> is the vertex at <m>(-2, 8)</m>. Therefore, <m>x =
                    -2</m>.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>

    <exercisegroup cols="2" xml:id="lesson-1-function-characteristics-table">
        <title>Function Characteristics</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>Use the letter or letters that describe the characteristic of the given functions.
                Some letters may be used more than once or not at all.</p>
            <p>
                <ul cols="2">
                    <li>
                        <p>A. <m>(-1,0)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>B. <m>[-1,0)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>C. <m>(-1,0]</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>D. <m>[-1,0]</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>E. <m>(0,-1)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>F. <m>[0,-1)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>G. <m>(0,-1]</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>H. <m>[0,-1]</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>I. <m>(-\infty,-1)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>J. <m>(-\infty,-1]</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>K. <m>(-1,\infty)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>L. <m>[-1,\infty)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>M. <m>(-\infty,\infty)</m></p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>N. Not given</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>Determine the <term>Domain</term>, <term>Range</term>, and <term>Intercepts</term>
                (List all that apply).</p>
        </introduction>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-function-characteristics-1" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-1-characteristics-1">
                    <caption>First Function</caption>
                    <image>
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane showing a solid curve drawn on a rectangular grid.
                                The x-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with numeric labels at -4,
                                -2, 2, and 4. The y-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with numeric
                                labels at -4, -2, and 2. The curve originates at a solid black dot
                                located exactly at the coordinates (-1, 0). From this starting
                                point, the curve extends continuously downward and to the right,
                                passing precisely through a second solid black dot at (0, -1). The
                                right end of the curve has an arrowhead, indicating that the path
                                continues indefinitely in the positive horizontal and negative
                                vertical directions.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                            label="prefig-domain-range-intercepts-1">
                            <diagram dimensions="(500,300)" margins="15">
                                <definition>f(x)=-1*sqrt(x+1)</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-5.5,-4.5,5.5,2.5)">
                                    <grid hticks="(-5,1,5)" vticks="(-4,1,2)" stroke="lightgray"
                                        spacings="((-5,1,5),(-4,1,2))" />
                                    <axes hticks="(-4,2,4)" vticks="(-4,2,2)" hlabels="(-4,2,4)"
                                        vlabels="(-4,2,2)" clear-background="yes" />
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(-1,5.4)" thickness="3" arrows="1" />
                                    <point p="(-1,0)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(0,-1)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Domain identifies valid <m>x</m>-values. Trace the graph horizontally from left
                    to right.</p>
                <p>Range identifies valid <m>y</m>-values. Trace the graph vertically from bottom to
                    top.</p>
                <p>Intercepts are coordinates where axes are crossed. Identify the exact points
                    where the curve touches or crosses the x-axis and y-axis.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Domain: L</p>
                <p>Range: N</p>
                <p>Intercepts: A, E</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>To determine the characteristics of the function, analyze the visual boundaries
                    of the given graph:</p>
                <p><term>Domain:</term> Trace the horizontal extent of the curve. The graph
                    originates at the solid point where <m>x = -1</m> and extends infinitely to the
                    right, indicated by the arrowhead. Thus, the domain consists of all real numbers
                    greater than or equal to <m>-1</m>, which is written as the interval <m>[-1,
                    \infty)</m>. This corresponds to option L.</p>
                <p><term>Range:</term> Trace the vertical extent of the curve. The highest point on
                    the curve is the solid point at <m>y = 0</m>, and the arrow indicates it
                    continues downwards towards <m>-\infty</m>. Thus, the range consists of all real
                    numbers less than or equal to <m>0</m>, which is written as the interval <m>(-\infty,
                    0]</m>. Because this interval is not one of the given choices, the correct
                    selection is N (Not given).</p>
                <p><term>Intercepts:</term> Locate the coordinates where the graph crosses the axes.
                    The graph originates exactly on the x-axis at the marked solid point <m>(-1, 0)</m>,
                    which is option A. The graph also crosses the y-axis at the explicitly marked
                    solid point <m>(0, -1)</m>, which is option E.</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="lesson-1-function-characteristics-2" workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <figure xml:id="fig-lesson-1-characteristics-2">
                    <caption>Second Function</caption>
                    <image>
                        <description>
                            <p>A coordinate plane showing a curve drawn on a rectangular grid. The
                                x-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with numeric labels at -2, 2,
                                and 4. The y-axis has grid lines every 1 unit, with numeric labels
                                at -4, -2, and 2. The curve originates at a solid black dot located
                                exactly at the coordinates (-1, -2). From this starting point, the
                                curve extends upward and to the right, reaching a peak at an open
                                circle (a hole) located exactly on the y-axis at the coordinates (0,
                                -1). From the open circle, the curve continues downward and to the
                                right in a parabolic shape. The right end of the curve has an
                                arrowhead, indicating that the path continues indefinitely in the
                                positive horizontal and negative vertical directions.</p>
                        </description>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
                            label="prefig-domain-range-intercepts-2">
                            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="15">
                                <definition>f(x)=-1*x^2-1</definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="(-2.5,-4.5,4.5,2.5)">
                                    <grid hticks="(-2,1,4)" vticks="(-4,1,2)" stroke="lightgray"
                                        spacings="((-2,1,4),(-4,1,2))" />
                                    <axes hticks="(-2,2,4)" vticks="(-4,2,2)" hlabels="(-2,2,4)"
                                        vlabels="(-4,2,2)" clear-background="yes" />
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(-1,1.8)" thickness="3" arrows="1"
                                        stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(-1,-2)" fill="blue" size="6" stroke="blue" />
                                    <point p="(0,-1)" fill="white" stroke="blue" thickness="2"
                                        size="6" />
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </figure>
            </statement>
            <hint>
                <p>Domain identifies valid <m>x</m>-values. Trace the graph horizontally from left
                    to right.</p>
                <p>Range identifies valid <m>y</m>-values. Trace the graph vertically from bottom to
                    top.</p>
                <p>Intercepts are coordinates where axes are crossed. Identify the exact points
                    where the curve touches or crosses the x-axis and y-axis.</p>
            </hint>
            <answer>
                <p>Domain: N</p>
                <p>Range: I</p>
                <p>Intercepts: N</p>
            </answer>
            <solution>
                <p>To determine the characteristics of the function, analyze the visual boundaries
                    of the given graph:</p>
                <p><term>Domain:</term> Trace the horizontal extent of the curve. The graph
                    originates at the solid point where <m>x = -1</m>, which means <m>-1</m> is
                    included. It extends to the right, but there is an open circle at <m>x = 0</m>,
                    meaning <m>0</m> is not part of the domain. The graph then continues infinitely
                    to the right, indicated by the arrowhead. Thus, the domain consists of all real
                    numbers greater than or equal to <m>-1</m>, excluding <m>0</m>. This is written
                    as the union of intervals <m>[-1, 0) \cup (0, \infty)</m>. Because this exact
                    interval is not one of the given choices, the correct selection is N (Not
                    given).</p>
                <p><term>Range:</term> Trace the vertical extent of the curve. The arrowhead
                    indicates the graph continues downwards towards <m>-\infty</m>. The highest
                    point on the curve is at the open circle where <m>y = -1</m>. Because it is an
                    open circle, the value <m>-1</m> is not included in the range. Thus, the range
                    consists of all real numbers strictly less than <m>-1</m>, which is written as
                    the interval <m>(-\infty, -1)</m>. This corresponds to option I.</p>
                <p><term>Intercepts:</term> Locate the coordinates where the graph crosses the axes.
                    The graph is entirely below the x-axis, so there are no x-intercepts. The graph
                    intersects the y-axis at the coordinate <m>(0, -1)</m>. However, because this is
                    an explicitly marked open circle, the function is undefined at this point.
                    Therefore, the graph has no valid y-intercept. Since there are no intercepts,
                    the correct selection is N (Not given).</p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercisegroup>
